	<main class="page article-shell">
		<header class="article-header">
			<h1 class="article-title">Why this lab exists</h1>
			<p class="lead">There is no shortage of opinions online. What is scarce is someone coming back afterwards to check the answer. Every call here gets a settlement date, and when it arrives the data decides. The ones that fail go to the top.</p>
		</header>
		<div class="prose">
			<h2>The problem this is trying to fix</h2>
			<p>Market commentary has a structural flaw: being right is remembered, being wrong is forgotten, and nobody keeps the books. The same person can look prescient year after year, because the misses are never dug up.</p>
			<p>So the idea here is simple — lock a judgement the moment it is written, along with the date it settles and the data that decides it. When the date arrives, check the answer. The ones that hold stay; the ones that are overturned go to the very top. Not out of unusual honesty, but because <strong>without doing this I cannot tell which of my own judgements are actually worth anything</strong>.</p>

			<h2>What is here</h2>
			<p>Research notes are the bulk of it — usually starting from an episode or a report, then recomputing the claim against my own data to see whether it holds. The <a href="/ledger/">ledger</a> holds calls that are locked and awaiting settlement (Chinese only for now). The <a href="/en/lab/">lab</a> has small interactive tools that turn a concept into something you can put your own numbers into. <a href="/en/projects/">Projects</a> collects code written for my own use that turned out to be useful to others.</p>
			<p>What is not here, stated plainly: <strong>no stock picks, no price targets, no individual recommendations</strong>. This is about how to judge, not what to buy.</p>

			<h2>Who writes this</h2>
			<p>I am Bocky, an individual investor with an engineering background. The most useful thing that training left me is not any particular technique — it is a habit: <strong>ask first what would make this wrong</strong>. A claim that cannot say how it would fail usually cannot say why it holds either.</p>
			<p>Public identity at <a href="https://github.com/bockybocky" rel="me">github.com/bockybocky</a> — code, sources and issues are all there. Read it, copy it, open a PR.</p>

			<h2>What the human does, what the AI does</h2>
			<p>This site is human-AI collaboration. It does not pretend to be entirely handwritten, nor entirely machine-generated.</p>
			<p>The split: <strong>direction, judgement and final calls are made by the human</strong>. The AI handles research legwork, code drafts, bilingual rewriting, tests and first drafts. Everything is reviewed by a human before it ships — the AI does not decide, it clears the grunt work and forces the thinking to get sharper. The English edition is a rewrite rather than a literal translation, also human-reviewed.</p>

			<h2>Say something</h2>
			<p>Every post takes comments. If you think a call is wrong, send a counter-example — the <a href="/propose/">challenge page</a> (Chinese only for now) takes them directly. Being overturned is a good outcome here; it is the entire point of the place.</p>

